WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.120
Film Courage: How many screenplays did you
write before FINAL DESTINATION?

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.730
Jeffrey Reddick: I think about seven.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.420
Film Courage: And these were features?

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.460
Jeffrey Reddick: Yes.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:17.050
Seven Features of varying degrees starting
with really awful to okay to good [Laughs].

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:22.470
Because I wrote my first one when I was in
middle school (no high school) and I sent

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.811
it to New Line Cinema and they were like “Oh,
it got great coverage but it’s not right

00:00:25.811 --> 00:00:26.811
for us.”

00:00:26.811 --> 00:00:29.810
And when I started at New Line like five years
later, I dug the coverage up and they were

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:30.880
like “This is awful!

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.859
It was obviously written by a middle schooler.”

00:00:32.859 --> 00:00:34.199
And I was like “I was in high school!”

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:40.670
So it’s interesting because I always tell
people you can’t take rejection when you

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.989
get it because if I were to take that coverage
in high school, I probably would have just

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:47.340
been like “Oh I don’t have any talent”
and I would have just given up.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:52.839
But you also have to be open to growing because
I also meet a lot of young people and God

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:55.449
Bless them but they also think that they are
already there.

00:00:55.449 --> 00:01:00.309
They are like “You know this is the best
script that Hollywood is ever going to make.”

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:01.309
And I’m like “You’re 15.

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:06.700
With all do respect, I’m sure it’s not
the best thing that Hollywood is ever going

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.700
to read.”

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.380
So you kind of need to let your ego go and
you have to grow.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:17.550
So I wrote a lot and just learning…it was
a learning process.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.300
But when I was young it was a lot of fun to
write.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.750
And then you start learning structure.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:30.590
And then when you start working at a studio
you start learning there’s a hard craft

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.140
that you need to kind of learn, too.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.060
So it’s mixing the business with the creative.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.460
Film Courage: And this was a script based
loosely on NIGHTMARE ON ELM STREET?

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.190
Jeffrey Reddick: That was a treatment I wrote
when I was 14 which got into…hello cat [stops

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:48.600
to pet cat]…see the little visitor here…that
I wrote for NIGHTMARE ON ELM STREET and that’s

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.150
what got me in touch with New Line Cinema
when I was 14.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:55.320
And I stayed in touch with them until I was
19 and I started interning there when I was

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:01.460
19 and they made and so they made FINAL DESTINATION
when I was age 30 (just to date myself).

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:06.420
But FINAL DESTINATION originally started out
as a spec script to get an agent for an X

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.890
FILES episode because they wanted you to write
something of your own and they also want you

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:15.320
to write something of your own so you can
show you can write in different character’s

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.320
voices.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.610
So I wrote an X FILES episode and I didn’t
submit it to the show because when my friends

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:24.870
at New Line said this would make a great feature
and then when I worked on it (ironically)

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:30.970
with two guys who worked on the X FILES, James
Wong and Glenn Morgan so I think it was kind

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.590
of a karmic thing that was meant to be with
the X FILES connection there.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.970
Film Courage: I’m curious though…what
is keeping in touch?

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.500
That’s fascinating to me as a 14 year-old
“Yeah, I’m just going to be keeping in

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.550
touch with New Line.”

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.550
[Laughs].

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.780
Jeffrey Reddick: Yeah well at first Bob Shay
he didn’t read my treatment because it was

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:53.120
unsolicited and then I wrote him kind of a
snarky…well not snarky but “Hey, I’ve

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.290
seen three of your movies and I’ve spent
money on you, I think you can take 5 minutes

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:57.310
to read my story.”

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.310
and so…

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.310
Film Courage: That’s great.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.310
I love it.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.720
Jeffrey Reddick: Yeah so he did and got back
to me and said…well his assistant (her name

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.690
was Joy Mann) she was just an amazing woman.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.690
She passed years ago unfortunately.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:13.810
And she was an amazing woman and she would
send me movie posters and little merchandising

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:18.950
things from movies and scripts to read and
I would stay in touch with them.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:23.050
They were always very gracious even to spend
time with me.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:26.281
And then one day, I don’t know if they even
knew if I was going to get to New York or

00:03:26.281 --> 00:03:29.900
not but they …I was pretty crafty…I should
be craftier.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.030
I’d make a good super villain if I ever
use my craftiness for evil but when I was

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:39.440
in college I had them start a special program
where I could go to New York to study acting

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.010
at the American Academy of Dramatic Arts and
then get credit for it.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.040
So the school paid for my whole trip to New
York my whole summer there.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.230
But then I got an internship at New Line when
I was there.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:55.590
I was like “Oh scr*w school” and decided
to stay in New York and stay at New Line.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.150
Film Courage: So they new you were age 16
or however old you were when you sent the

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.150
script in?

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.910
Jeffrey Riddick: Well I think Joy…I don’t
know if I ever told Joy how old I was.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:06.970
I think she asked me once.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.190
Because I would call her, too.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.650
I got the phone number from information and
she would talk to me.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.120
Film Courage: That’s great.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.479
Jeffrey Riddick: Yeah…and this was back
when New Line was…I mean NIGHTMARE ON ELM

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:19.669
STREET had come out so they were definitely
growing but they hadn’t turned into like

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:23.629
the powerhouse that they became.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:27.729
But definitely that wouldn’t happen today.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:29.370
It was a real blessing.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:35.199
I always give credit to Bob and Joy for…if
they hadn’t have stayed in touch with me

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:40.430
and inspired me and then brought me on as
an intern when I first started, when I went

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.639
to New York, I mean I’m sure I still would
have been in the business, it just was my

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:50.559
path I think but they were really crucial.

00:04:50.559 --> 00:04:54.590
Warner Brothers absorbed New Line so it’s
not probably the same place anymore, but it

00:04:54.590 --> 00:05:03.779
probably was one of the best studios to be
built because it was so artist friendly and

00:05:03.779 --> 00:05:05.789
they really cared.

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:07.580
Bob Shay was a huge film lover.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.819
Like he started distribution John Waters films
out of his apartment to college campuses and

00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:12.819
doing these tours.

00:05:12.819 --> 00:05:16.539
So he’s a huge film lover so he created
this company that is a huge company filled

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:18.259
with film lovers.

00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:20.969
And now you have a lot of great companies
but a lot of them are run by business people

00:05:20.969 --> 00:05:22.699
who don’t love film.

00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:27.970
So they understand the equations of so if
I put this formula together it will make money

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.069
but they don’t understand the creative side
of it.

00:05:31.069 --> 00:05:36.330
And New Line was just a wonderful fostering
environment to work.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.990
So yeah…I got really lucky with that one.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:42.030
Film Courage: You first year there [at New
Line] you were age 19?

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.030
Jeffrey Riddick: Yes.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.270
Film Couarge: Do you remember what that was
like because it must have been surreal to

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.509
work with this group of people where first
they turned you down but then they keep in

00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:52.539
touch with you and send you things and now
you are there [working]?

00:05:52.539 --> 00:06:00.309
Jeffrey Riddick: It was surreal in a way but
it’s a weird thing just because I always

00:06:00.309 --> 00:06:03.419
knew I was going to be working in the movie
business some how.

00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:10.129
It didn’t hit me super…but it did hit
me sometimes when I found that coverage for

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:11.129
the first thing I wrote.

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:14.400
I was like “Wow, if I would have read this,
if they had told me the truth I might not

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.400
be here.”

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:21.400
But because it was all happening and again
when you’re younger you’re still at that

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.580
“I can do anything in the world and nothing
can stop me stage.”

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:33.279
So I have always been so appreciative of the
opportunity but I wasn’t like ever surprised

00:06:33.279 --> 00:06:36.430
but it was really cool because I got to meet
all the people from NIGHTMARE ON ELM STREET.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:40.729
Meeting all these people that I had grown
up idolizing was like cool and I remember

00:06:40.729 --> 00:06:47.099
we were at the SEVEN premiere, the amazing
movie with Brad Pitt and Morgan Freeman and

00:06:47.099 --> 00:06:53.460
all of the sudden I am talking to Morgan Freeman
for like an hour and so they teach you when

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.550
you work there don’t act weird when the
celebrities come in because they would come

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:57.649
in there all the time for meetings.

00:06:57.649 --> 00:07:01.710
You learn how to shut out that geeky part
of your brain.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:07.669
But then I had lunch with Jane Badler who
was in V the miniseries that came out in the

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:13.199
80’s who played Diana the evil, lizard,
alien queen but I had lunched with her.

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:16.409
And that was the first time I had actually
been star struck where I couldn’t talk because

00:07:16.409 --> 00:07:20.749
I didn’t know what star struck meant until
I had lunch with her and I was like I can’t

00:07:20.749 --> 00:07:30.159
talk right now and this is really embarrassing
and I’m a grown man and why am I like this?

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:35.009
Again it was a wonderful thing and it was
a wonderful convergence of the right people

00:07:35.009 --> 00:07:37.309
like Bob and Joy taking time.

00:07:37.309 --> 00:07:41.330
Because Bob could have just written in that
one thing and written me off and Joy could

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:43.599
have been like “Oh, that’s cute.”

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:47.819
But she would stay in touch with me and I
probably just bugged the hell out of her.

00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:50.509
I was always like “Can you read this?”

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:56.919
And she was just very patient and it was a
wonderful experience, it really was.

